## TL;DR — 5 Asscher Cut Diamond Proportion Specs to Check

**The 5 specs that expose a bad asscher cut diamond — check these on every certificate before buying:**

1. **Depth %: 60–68%** — below 60% creates a shallow, windowed stone; above 68% looks dark and heavy
2. **Table %: 60–68%** — below 60% tightens the center octagon; above 70% flattens the face-up appearance
3. **Length-to-Width Ratio: 1.00–1.05** — above 1.05 the stone looks rectangular, not square
4. **Polish: Excellent or Very Good** — step-cut facets show polish defects that brilliant cuts hide
5. **Symmetry: Excellent or Very Good** — any misalignment in asscher's concentric facets breaks the corridor effect

**GIA does not grade cut quality on asscher cut diamonds.** The "Ideal" label on any asscher is the vendor's own assessment, not GIA's. These 5 numbers on the certificate are the only objective check you have.

**Best proportion-verified natural entry:** → [GIA 1.00ct E-VS1 at $2,464](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/28919719?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) — always verify depth/table on the certificate before purchasing any specific stone.

**Best proportion-verified lab entry:** → [IGI 1.52ct D-IF at $2,795](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/27991043?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational)

---

[Asscher cut diamond proportions](/asscher-cut-diamond) are the most important thing to check before buying — and the thing most buyers never look at. They check color, clarity, and carat weight. They assume the cut label handles the rest.

It doesn't. GIA does not grade cut quality on asscher cut diamonds — the “Ideal” label on every listing is the vendor's self-assessment. Two identically-graded asschers can have wildly different proportions and look completely different in a ring.

This post gives you the exact numbers: what depth percentage to spec, what table percentage to spec, what L/W ratio to look for, what polish and symmetry grades to require. It explains what happens to the stone when each number falls outside the ideal range. And it shows you real certified market data so you can cross-check price against grade and proportion quality.

---

## Why Asscher Cut Proportions Are Different from Everything Else

For round brilliant diamonds, GIA issues a full cut grade — Excellent, Very Good, Good, Fair, Poor. That grade accounts for depth, table, culet, girdle thickness, crown angle, pavilion angle, and more. When you buy a GIA Excellent round brilliant, you know the cut has been independently verified.

Asscher cut diamonds do not receive this grade — GIA's cut grading system applies only to round brilliant diamonds. For all other shapes, including asscher, emerald, oval, and cushion, GIA grades color and clarity only. Cut quality is ungraded.

This is not widely known, and it's the reason buyers get burned. They see "GIA Certified, Ideal Cut" on a listing and assume GIA verified the cut — it didn't. GIA certified color and clarity; the vendor decided "Ideal" on their own.

IGI does issue cut grades for fancy shapes including asscher. But even IGI's cut grade is based on proportion data you can verify yourself — the grade is a summary, not a substitute. The numbers are the truth.

This is why [asscher cut diamond proportions](/asscher-cut-diamond) are the buyer's most important due-diligence step — and why this post exists.

---

## 5 Specs That Expose a Bad Asscher Cut Diamond

### Spec 1 — Depth Percentage: 60–68%

Depth percentage is the total height of the stone divided by its average width, expressed as a percentage. It is the single most important proportion number for an asscher cut diamond.

**Below 60%:** The stone is too shallow. Light enters the table, travels through a shallow pavilion, and leaks out the bottom instead of reflecting back up. The result is a "window" — a transparent patch where you look straight through the stone instead of seeing the hall-of-mirrors corridor effect. Shallow asschers look dull and glassy face-up.

**60–68%:** The ideal range. Light enters and bounces between the pavilion facets correctly, reflecting back through the table and creating the deep rectangular corridors of the hall-of-mirrors effect. This is the range that makes an asscher look alive.

**Above 68%:** The stone is too deep. Light gets trapped inside the pavilion and absorbs rather than reflects. The stone looks dark at the center. It also carries excess weight below the girdle — you pay for carat weight that doesn't show on the finger. A 1ct stone with 72% depth faces up smaller than a 1ct stone with 64% depth.

| Depth % | Visual result | What this means |
|---|---|---|
| Below 58% | Severe window — transparency through the table | Never buy; the hall-of-mirrors effect is destroyed |
| 58–60% | Mild window, reduced brilliance | Borderline — view in person before purchasing |
| 60–68% | Ideal — full corridor effect | This is the target range for every asscher buyer |
| 68–72% | Dark center, weight penalty | Mediocre; paying for carat weight that doesn't show |
| Above 72% | Extremely dark, heavy, poorly performing | Avoid entirely |

**Verdict:** If the depth is not between 60–68%, stop. No color or clarity grade compensates for a stone with a window or a dark center. Depth is non-negotiable.

---

### Spec 2 — Table Percentage: 60–68%

Table percentage is the width of the flat top facet divided by the average width of the stone. In an asscher, the table facet defines the center octagon — the window into the step-cut corridors.

**Below 60%:** The table is small. The center octagon looks tight and cramped. Less light enters the crown, and the corridor effect is narrow. The stone looks more closed-off than open. Some buyers prefer a smaller table for an antique aesthetic, but below 60% is generally considered too small for modern asscher cuts.

**60–68%:** The ideal range. The center octagon is proportionate to the outline, light enters efficiently, and the corridors appear deep and well-defined. The eight-pointed star in a well-proportioned asscher forms cleanly.

**Above 70%:** The table is too large and the crown is too flat. Instead of a clearly defined octagonal center, you see a large flat surface — the hall-of-mirrors depth diminishes. The stone looks spread and flat rather than deep and architectural.

| Table % | Visual result | What this means |
|---|---|---|
| Below 58% | Cramped center octagon, dim entry | Antique look but too restricted — corridor effect weakened |
| 58–60% | Small table, moderate corridor effect | Borderline acceptable — check alongside depth |
| 60–68% | Ideal — proportionate octagon, deep corridors | The target for all buyers |
| 68–72% | Oversized table, flatter appearance | Crown height is low; step-cut loses depth |
| Above 72% | Very flat, spread appearance | Avoid — resembles a flat mirror rather than a corridor |

**Verdict:** Table and depth work together. A 65% table with 65% depth is the sweet spot. An 67% table with 62% depth still performs well. If both are within range, the stone performs correctly. If one is out of range, the other cannot compensate.

---

### Spec 3 — Length-to-Width Ratio: 1.00–1.05

An asscher cut diamond should look square from above. The length-to-width ratio tells you how close to square it actually is. A true 1:1 ratio is a perfect square. A 1.05 ratio is nearly square but slightly elongated — typically visible only with close inspection.

**Below 1.00:** This would mean the stone is wider than it is long — unusual and typically a cut defect. Avoid.

**1.00–1.05:** The accepted range. At 1.00, the stone is a perfect square; at 1.05, there is a very slight elongation. Most buyers cannot see the difference between 1.00 and 1.04 with the naked eye.

**Above 1.05:** The stone starts to look noticeably rectangular rather than square. The characteristic octagonal asscher outline shifts toward a rectangle. This is acceptable if you specifically want a slightly elongated look, but most asscher buyers want the square silhouette that defines the cut.

| L/W Ratio | Appearance | What this means |
|---|---|---|
| 1.00 | Perfect square | Classic asscher look — most sought-after |
| 1.01–1.03 | Virtually square | Imperceptible to the naked eye — excellent |
| 1.04–1.05 | Nearly square | Slight elongation; most buyers accept this range |
| 1.06–1.08 | Slightly rectangular | Visible elongation — personal preference, not a defect |
| Above 1.08 | Noticeably rectangular | Starts to resemble an emerald cut silhouette |

**Verdict:** Target 1.00–1.05. If the ratio is above 1.05, ask to see the stone in person or request a video. For some buyers, a 1.07 ratio creates an appealing slightly elongated asscher. For buyers who want the true square, stay at or below 1.05.

---

### Spec 4 — Polish Grade: Excellent or Very Good

Polish refers to the surface smoothness of each facet. In a round brilliant, minor polish defects get lost in the constant sparkle and movement. In an asscher cut, each facet is large and flat — a transparency layer into the stone. Surface defects on a step-cut facet are visible in a way that a brilliant-cut's small triangular facets would never show.

Polish is graded: Excellent, Very Good, Good, Fair, Poor. Accept only Excellent or Very Good for asscher cut diamonds.

A "Good" polish grade on an asscher can mean surface scratches, burn marks from polishing, or lizard skin texture visible under magnification. In a round brilliant at 10x magnification, these are irrelevant. In an asscher viewed across its flat table, they scatter light in ways that reduce the depth and clarity of the corridor effect.

**Why this matters in price:** Two stones with identical color and clarity grades can differ by hundreds of dollars if one has Good polish and the other has Excellent. Buyers who don't know to check polish may pay for a stone with visual degradation they can't name.

---

### Spec 5 — Symmetry Grade: Excellent or Very Good

Symmetry measures how precisely the facets are aligned relative to each other and to the girdle plane. For most diamond cuts, symmetry affects sparkle pattern. For asscher cut diamonds, symmetry affects something more fundamental: the architecture of the hall-of-mirrors effect.

The asscher's corridor effect requires its concentric rectangular facets to be perfectly parallel and evenly spaced. If the facets are misaligned — even slightly — the corridor reflections are disrupted, giving you distorted, uneven light instead of clean corridors. The stone looks busy and confused rather than deep and architectural.

Accept only Excellent or Very Good symmetry. A "Good" symmetry grade on an asscher is a meaningful visual downgrade.

| Grade | Brilliant cut impact | Asscher cut impact | What this means |
|---|---|---|---|
| Excellent | Ideal sparkle pattern | Perfect corridor alignment | Buy this |
| Very Good | Nearly ideal | Minor alignment variance, invisible to naked eye | Acceptable |
| Good | Minor sparkle disruption | Visible disruption to corridor effect | Avoid on asscher |
| Fair | Noticeable sparkle issues | Significant corridor distortion | Never buy |
| Poor | Obvious defects | Completely broken corridor structure | Never buy |

**Verdict:** Symmetry Excellent or Very Good, no exceptions. This is especially critical at 2ct+ where the step facets are physically larger and any misalignment covers more visual area.

