## The Vendor's Ideal Problem

Here is the thing no one tells you when you're shopping for an [asscher cut diamond](/asscher-cut-diamond): the "Ideal" label on every listing is not from GIA. It is not verified by any independent grading body. It is the retailer's own classification.

GIA grades round brilliant diamonds on cut quality — for all other shapes including asscher, GIA issues no cut grade. The certificate shows color, clarity, carat weight, and measurements only. The cut quality line on a GIA certificate for a fancy shape reads "Not Applicable."

This creates a gap that retailers fill with their own language. "Ideal Cut" on an asscher means the retailer decided the stone meets their internal standard — different retailers set that bar differently. Some use the 60–68% depth range; others call anything without obvious defects "Ideal."

The result: two GIA-certified asschers with identical color, clarity, and carat weight can look completely different, and both can be labeled "Ideal." The only way to know which one actually performs is to check the proportion data on the certificate yourself.

Here is a real-world illustration from certified inventory. These are two GIA E-VS1 natural asscher diamonds at approximately 1ct:

| Stone | Grade | Price | Note |
|---|---|---|---|
| [GIA 1.00ct E-VS1](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/28919719?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) | E-VS1 | [$2,464](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/28919719?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) | Always verify depth/table on the actual GIA report |
| [GIA 1.02ct E-VS1](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/17546196?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) | E-VS1 | [$4,360](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/17546196?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) | Both labeled "Ideal" — $1,896 price gap reflects proportion and polish differences |

**Verdict:** The $1,896 gap between two "Ideal" E-VS1 stones at essentially the same carat weight tells you everything. Proportions, polish, and symmetry — verified on the actual GIA report, not the vendor's label — are what determine this difference. Always request or view the full GIA certificate before purchasing.

---

> **Farzana says:** "I've had clients come to me with an asscher they bought online — 'GIA certified, Ideal cut.' They paid for Ideal. They got a dark, heavy stone with 71% depth that killed the hall-of-mirrors effect. The vendor wasn't lying. Their 'Ideal' just meant something different than what the buyer thought it meant. Always check the three numbers: depth, table, and L/W ratio. Don't buy the label."

