## How to Read an Asscher Cut Diamond Certificate — Step by Step

### GIA Certificate (Natural Asscher)

1. **Find the Shape and Cutting Style line** — should say "Asscher" or "Square Emerald" (GIA terminology varies; square emerald = asscher)
2. **Find the Measurements** — listed as Length × Width × Depth in millimeters. Divide depth by the average of length and width to confirm the depth percentage
3. **Find the Depth % line** — GIA lists this directly. Target: 60.0–68.0%
4. **Find the Table % line** — GIA lists this directly. Target: 60–68%
5. **Find the Polish and Symmetry lines** — both should read "Excellent" or "Very Good"
6. **Calculate L/W Ratio** — divide the longer measurement by the shorter one. Target: 1.00–1.05
7. **Ignore the Cut line** — it says "Not Applicable." This is not a mistake. GIA does not grade asscher cut quality.

### IGI Certificate (Lab Asscher)

1. **Find the Shape** — should say "Asscher Cut" or "Square Step Cut"
2. **Find the Cut Grade** — IGI does issue this for fancy shapes. Excellent or Very Good is required
3. **Find Depth % and Table %** — same targets: 60–68% each
4. **Find Polish and Symmetry** — Excellent or Very Good
5. **Calculate L/W Ratio** from the measurements line
6. **Verify the Lab-Grown notation** — should explicitly state "Lab-Grown Diamond" or "Laboratory Grown Diamond" and note CVD or HPHT growth method

---

## Asscher Cut Diamond Proportions Optimization Matrix

Use this matrix to diagnose any asscher certificate in under two minutes.

| Spec | Ideal Range | Yellow Zone | Red Zone | What this means |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Depth % | 60–68% | 58–60% or 68–70% | Below 58% or above 70% | Yellow zone: inspect in person first. Red zone: reject. |
| Table % | 60–68% | 58–60% or 68–70% | Below 58% or above 70% | Same logic as depth — both affect corridor effect equally |
| L/W Ratio | 1.00–1.05 | 1.05–1.08 | Above 1.08 | Yellow zone: buy only if you want elongated look. Red zone: avoid. |
| Polish | Excellent / Very Good | Good | Fair or Poor | Good polish is visible on step-cut flat facets — don't accept it |
| Symmetry | Excellent / Very Good | Good | Fair or Poor | Good symmetry breaks the corridor alignment — don't accept it |
| Culet | None or Pointed | Very Small | Small or larger | A culet (bottom point) is visible through step-cut facets at the center |
| Fluorescence | None to Faint | Medium | Strong or Very Strong | Strong fluorescence can cause haze in step-cut facets under UV light |

**Verdict:** Any "Red Zone" result on any spec is a rejection. "Yellow Zone" specs require in-person viewing or a video from the vendor before committing. A stone that passes all specs in the Ideal Range is a correctly cut asscher — regardless of what the vendor calls it.

---

## Final Verdict — What to Buy Based on Proportions

### Best for Natural Buyers Prioritizing Proportions First

Start at [GIA 1.00ct E-VS1 at $2,464](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/28919719?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) as your price benchmark. Request the full GIA report number and verify depth (60–68%), table (60–68%), L/W ratio (1.00–1.05), Polish (EX/VG), and Symmetry (EX/VG) before any purchase. A stone that passes all five specs at this price is a correctly cut 1ct natural asscher. See the [asscher cut diamond engagement rings guide](/asscher-cut-diamond-engagement-rings) for setting recommendations that complement the proportions.

### Best for Natural Buyers at 2ct

[GIA 2.02ct E-VVS2 at $26,344](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/22977740?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) is the recommended 2ct natural spec. At $26K, you have zero tolerance for proportion failures. Request the GIA certificate number, verify all five specs directly on the GIA website, and check that depth is 60–68% before paying.

### Best for Lab Buyers Prioritizing Proportions

[IGI 2.29ct D-IF at $5,061](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/27991135?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) with IGI Excellent or Very Good cut grade. Verify the underlying depth and table on the IGI report. At $5K, this stone with correct proportions is a better visual result than a natural 2ct asscher with poor proportions at $21,000. Proportion quality beats price in every case. See the full [lab grown asscher cut diamond guide](/lab-grown-asscher-cut-diamond) for the complete buying framework.

### Best for Buyers Seeing Two "Identical" Stones at Different Prices

If you find two GIA asschers with the same color, clarity, and carat weight but different prices — the cheaper one almost certainly has at least one out-of-range proportion. Pull both certificates. Compare depth, table, L/W, polish, and symmetry. The spec difference will explain the price difference every time.

---

## Expert Summary

Asscher cut diamond proportions are the one thing GIA won't verify for you. The "Ideal" cut label means nothing on an asscher — it is the vendor's own assessment, not an independent grade. The five specs in this post — depth, table, L/W ratio, polish, and symmetry — are the objective standard that replaces the missing GIA cut grade.

A stone that hits all five specs produces the full hall-of-mirrors corridor effect that makes asscher cut diamonds extraordinary. A stone that fails even one spec will be dark, windowed, flat, or architecturally distorted — regardless of its color, clarity, or the "Ideal" label it carries.

The [asscher cut diamond pillar page](/asscher-cut-diamond) covers the full buying picture including pricing, clarity rules, and settings. The [loose asscher cut diamond guide](/loose-asscher-cut-diamond) shows natural vs lab prices side by side if you're still deciding on origin.

Two minutes on the certificate. Five numbers. That's the difference between a stone that takes your breath away and one that never quite looks right.

