## Frequently Asked Questions

### What are the ideal proportions for an asscher cut diamond?

The ideal asscher cut diamond proportions are: depth 60–68%, table 60–68%, length-to-width ratio 1.00–1.05, polish Excellent or Very Good, symmetry Excellent or Very Good. All five must fall within range for the stone to produce the full hall-of-mirrors corridor effect that defines the asscher cut.

### What depth percentage is ideal for an asscher cut diamond?

The ideal depth percentage for an asscher cut diamond is 60–68%. Below 60%, the stone develops a transparent window where light leaks through the bottom. Above 68%, the center appears dark and the stone carries hidden weight below the girdle. Depth is the single most important proportion number for asscher cut performance.

### What table percentage should I look for in an asscher cut diamond?

The ideal table percentage for an asscher cut diamond is 60–68%. A table below 60% creates a cramped, tight center octagon that restricts light entry. A table above 70% flattens the crown and reduces the three-dimensional depth of the corridor effect. Table and depth should both fall in range simultaneously.

### What is the ideal length-to-width ratio for an asscher cut diamond?

The ideal length-to-width ratio for an asscher cut diamond is 1.00–1.05. At 1.00, the stone is a perfect square. At 1.05, there is a slight elongation that most buyers cannot detect with the naked eye. Above 1.05 the stone begins to look rectangular rather than square, moving toward an emerald cut silhouette.

### Does GIA grade the cut quality of asscher cut diamonds?

No. GIA's cut grading system applies only to round brilliant diamonds. For all fancy shapes — including asscher cut — the Cut grade on a GIA certificate says "Not Applicable." Every "Ideal" or "Excellent" cut label you see on a natural asscher cut diamond is the vendor's own classification, not a GIA designation. Check the proportion data directly.

### What is the difference between "Ideal" cut on an asscher vs a round brilliant?

On a round brilliant, "Ideal" cut means GIA independently verified the stone's proportions, polish, and symmetry meet the highest standard. On an asscher cut diamond, "Ideal" means the vendor decided the stone looks good to them. It is self-certified with no independent standard behind it. This is why verifying the five proportion specs yourself is mandatory for asscher.

### How do I read an asscher cut diamond certificate for proportions?

On a GIA certificate, find the Depth % and Table % lines — these are listed directly. Find Polish and Symmetry grades in the Finish section. Calculate L/W ratio from the measurements (divide longer dimension by shorter). For IGI lab asscher certificates, additionally check the Cut grade. Verify the GIA report number at gia.edu or IGI report at igi.org before purchasing.

### What polish and symmetry grade do I need for an asscher cut diamond?

Excellent or Very Good for both polish and symmetry. A "Good" polish grade means surface imperfections are present that will scatter light on the asscher's large flat facets — visible in ways that would be invisible on a round brilliant. A "Good" symmetry grade means the concentric step facets are misaligned, which breaks the corridor effect. Never accept Good or below on either.

### What happens if depth is too deep on an asscher cut diamond?

A depth above 68% causes the center of the stone to appear dark. Light that enters the table travels through an overly deep pavilion, reflects at an angle that sends it back through the side rather than up through the table, and is lost. The stone also carries excess carat weight below the girdle — you pay for size that doesn't show on the finger.

### What happens if the table is too large on an asscher cut diamond?

A table above 70% flattens the crown. The crown height is what creates the three-dimensional depth of the hall-of-mirrors effect — a low crown means shallow corridors. The stone looks like a flat, spread mirror rather than a deep architectural structure. The center octagon loses its visual definition.

### How do proportions affect the hall-of-mirrors effect in asscher cut?

The hall-of-mirrors effect — long rectangular light corridors that appear to recede into the stone — requires that light enter the crown at the correct angle (determined by depth), bounce between correctly positioned pavilion facets (determined by depth and symmetry), and exit cleanly through a properly sized table (determined by table %). When all specs align, the corridors form. When any one is out of range, the corridors degrade or disappear.

### How do I compare two asscher cut diamonds with the same color and clarity?

Pull both certificates. Compare depth %, table %, L/W ratio, polish, and symmetry. The stone with all specs in range (60–68% depth, 60–68% table, 1.00–1.05 L/W, EX/VG polish and symmetry) will outperform the stone with out-of-range specs regardless of their identical color and clarity. Price differences between identically-graded stones almost always reflect proportion differences.

### What proportions should a lab grown asscher cut diamond have?

Identical to natural: depth 60–68%, table 60–68%, L/W 1.00–1.05, polish Excellent/Very Good, symmetry Excellent/Very Good. Lab origin does not change the optics of a step-cut diamond. A lab D-IF asscher with 72% depth looks dark and heavy — the same visual failure as a natural D-IF with 72% depth. Check the [lab grown asscher cut diamond guide](/lab-grown-asscher-cut-diamond) for the full spec and pricing framework.

### Why is symmetry especially important for asscher cut diamonds?

Asscher's hall-of-mirrors effect depends on its concentric rectangular facets being precisely parallel and evenly spaced. Any misalignment — even small enough to receive a "Good" symmetry grade — disrupts the corridor alignment. Instead of clean, deep corridors, you see distorted, uneven reflections. On a round brilliant, minor symmetry issues are lost in the sparkle pattern. On an asscher's flat, transparent facets, they are structural.

